const faq = [
  { q: 'What does the raven symbolise in Slavic mythology?', a: 'In Slavic folklore the raven (Russian and many other Slavic languages: voron) carries multiple symbolic registers — prophecy and foreknowledge, death and war, transformation, and the possession of hidden or magical knowledge. The raven is often associated with Veles, the Slavic deity of the underworld, cattle, wealth, and magic. It appears in byliny (epic oral poems), fairy tales (skazki), and folk beliefs as a liminal creature connected to death and the Other World.' },
  { q: 'Is the raven good or bad in Slavic folklore?', a: 'The raven in Slavic tradition is morally ambivalent — not simply good or evil. It possesses knowledge and power, but that power is not inherently benign. Ravens are associated with death and the underworld, which means encountering one can be an ill omen (particularly in folk belief about predicting deaths), but ravens can also be helpers, guides, or messengers for heroes in fairy tales. The ambivalence mirrors the broader Slavic cosmological duality between the upper world (Prav) and the lower world (Nav).' },
